WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.054 --> 00:00:09.001
FAYE MOSS: Hello, everyone, and welcome to Superstars in History , the talk show where we meet famous people from

2
00:00:09.002 --> 00:00:14.559
the past. Today's guest was the longest-serving first lady of the United

3
00:00:14.560 --> 00:00:24.785
States. While expanding the modern role of the president's wife from hostess to activist, she broke boundaries and encouraged change.

4
00:00:24.786 --> 00:00:45.195
Straight from history, please welcome Eleanor Roosevelt.

5
00:00:45.196 --> 00:00:47.127
ELEANOR ROOSEVELT:

6
00:00:47.128 --> 00:00:51.864
Hello, Faye! FAYE MOSS: It's amazing that you're related

7
00:00:51.865 --> 00:00:56.488
to two presidents! Your uncle Theodore Roosevelt was president in

8
00:00:56.489 --> 00:01:03.496
the early 1900s, and your husband, Franklin Delano Roosevelt, also known as FDR, later

9
00:01:03.497 --> 00:01:12.428
became president. You were born into a wealthy, socially prominent New York family in 1884, but you didn't have the happiest

10
00:01:12.429 --> 00:01:20.587
childhood. ELEANOR ROOSEVELT: I disappointed my beautiful mother because I was a shy, unattractive child who was afraid

11
00:01:20.588 --> 00:01:26.685
of everything. Before I was ten, both my parents died, so I was raised by my

12
00:01:26.686 --> 00:01:33.489
grandmother. Other children thought I was a strange, sad girl whose dresses were always out of

13
00:01:33.490 --> 00:01:39.696
style. I felt like an outsider and hated that everyone pitied me because I was an orphan.

14
00:01:39.697 --> 00:01:45.473
FAYE MOSS: How did you go from awkward child to activist first

15
00:01:45.474 --> 00:01:53.518
lady? ELEANOR ROOSEVELT: When I was 15, my grandmother sent me to a girls' school near London, where I spent three

16
00:01:53.519 --> 00:02:01.325
happy years. The headmistress, Mademoiselle Souvestre, encouraged and inspired us to do something with our

17
00:02:01.326 --> 00:02:10.091
lives. I wanted to stay another year, but my grandmother insisted I return to New York City to make my entry into society.

18
00:02:10.092 --> 00:02:15.418
FAYE MOSS: Oh, I'd love to dress up and go to all those

19
00:02:15.419 --> 00:02:25.843
fancy balls! ELEANOR ROOSEVELT: I hated that! Thanks to Mademoiselle Souvestre, I knew there was more to life than

20
00:02:25.844 --> 00:02:33.952
finding a husband. I taught classes in the New York City slums and investigated the poor working conditions of women

21
00:02:33.953 --> 00:02:38.163
and children. FAYE MOSS: It sounds like you were busy! How did you

22
00:02:38.164 --> 00:02:46.166
meet your husband? ELEANOR ROOSEVELT: Franklin was a distant cousin, so we'd met as children before we encountered each other

23
00:02:46.167 --> 00:02:52.797
again as young adults. We married in 1905 and had six children, though one died

24
00:02:52.798 --> 00:02:56.688
as an infant. FAYE MOSS: That was only

25
00:02:56.689 --> 00:03:02.300
one crisis you faced together. ELEANOR ROOSEVELT: In 1921 Franklin

26
00:03:02.301 --> 00:03:05.315
contracted polio and was suddenly paralyzed.

27
00:03:05.316 --> 00:03:13.686
He never walked again without assistance. His mother wanted him to return to his childhood home and become a country gentleman, but I wasn't

28
00:03:13.687 --> 00:03:18.248
going to let that happen. Before his illness, he'd planned to run for governor

29
00:03:18.249 --> 00:03:24.000
of New York State. I kept his name alive in the Democratic Party by encouraging politicians

30
00:03:24.001 --> 00:03:28.991
to visit him. I also rose to leadership positions in the women's division

31
00:03:28.992 --> 00:03:34.458
of the party. It was a busy time because I was teaching at a girls' school in New

32
00:03:34.459 --> 00:03:42.992
York City. I kept teaching after Franklin was elected governor in 1928, and we had moved to the state capital several hours

33
00:03:42.993 --> 00:03:46.416
away. I also did some writing and radio work.

34
00:03:46.417 --> 00:03:54.896
FAYE MOSS: When FDR became president in 1933, the Great Depression had left millions of Americans jobless and

35
00:03:54.897 --> 00:04:05.621
homeless. FDR believed the government should take care of people, so he created the New Deal—an ambitious package of programs to employ and help them.

36
00:04:05.622 --> 00:04:09.508
ELEANOR ROOSEVELT: I'm impressed with how much

37
00:04:09.509 --> 00:04:13.942
you know! FAYE MOSS: Thanks! How did you feel about

38
00:04:13.943 --> 00:04:21.578
becoming first lady? ELEANOR ROOSEVELT: I was reluctant because I dreaded becoming just a hostess, and I'd avoided this

39
00:04:21.579 --> 00:04:27.369
role my whole life. Having earned my own money, I didn't want to give up my work and

40
00:04:27.370 --> 00:04:38.382
my financial independence. Some people criticized me for hosting radio programs, writing magazine articles, and giving speeches—even though I donated my earnings

41
00:04:38.383 --> 00:04:44.956
to charity. FAYE MOSS: It was unusual back then for married women to work, never mind

42
00:04:44.957 --> 00:04:47.832
the first lady. ELEANOR ROOSEVELT: I

43
00:04:47.833 --> 00:04:56.379
always encouraged women to work. For example, to encourage newspapers to employ women reporters, only women were allowed to

44
00:04:56.380 --> 00:05:01.877
attend my press conferences. FAYE MOSS: You were also well known as a civil rights

45
00:05:01.878 --> 00:05:10.033
champion. How did that start? ELEANOR ROOSEVELT: As first lady I traveled the country to gather information so I could update

46
00:05:10.034 --> 00:05:13.910
Franklin on Americans' opinions and needs. I was never afraid to tell him what

47
00:05:13.911 --> 00:05:21.933
I thought he should hear. In 1933, after visiting unemployed coal miners, I pushed for the

48
00:05:21.934 --> 00:05:28.404
development of Arthurdale, West Virginia. This community provided jobs as well as decent housing for

49
00:05:28.405 --> 00:05:35.302
miners and their families. Although many people judged it a failure because of its cost, I was happy that we could

50
00:05:35.303 --> 00:05:42.662
help some families. But I was disappointed when the first residents banned African Americans from

51
00:05:42.663 --> 00:05:48.115
the community. This led me to become more involved in the fight against racial

52
00:05:48.116 --> 00:05:54.381
prejudice. FAYE MOSS: Speaking of which, tell us about the famous incident with

53
00:05:54.382 --> 00:06:02.235
singer Marian Anderson. ELEANOR ROOSEVELT: In 1939 she wasn't allowed to perform at a Washington, DC, auditorium because

54
00:06:02.236 --> 00:06:05.944
she was African American. I spoke against the organization that

55
00:06:05.945 --> 00:06:16.148
owned the venue and helped arrange an outdoor concert at the Lincoln Memorial that 75,000 people attended—much more than the original venue

56
00:06:16.149 --> 00:06:25.620
could hold! FAYE MOSS: That's incredible! During World War II, you identified with many mothers because your four sons were serving

57
00:06:25.621 --> 00:06:32.026
in the military. Your visits to American troops in the South Pacific and Great Britain increased

58
00:06:32.027 --> 00:06:38.557
your popularity. Journalists and high-ranking military men were amazed at your energy

59
00:06:38.558 --> 00:06:47.886
and stamina. ELEANOR ROOSEVELT: During my trips I was also working on "My Day," my six-day-a-week newspaper column that I

60
00:06:47.887 --> 00:06:54.102
started in 1936. Over the years I wrote about everything from places I visited to issues I wanted

61
00:06:54.103 --> 00:07:02.220
to push. When the British king and queen visited us before the war, I wrote about people criticizing me for serving hot

62
00:07:02.221 --> 00:07:05.853
dogs. They loved the hot dogs, by the way!

63
00:07:05.854 --> 00:07:15.652
FAYE MOSS: Your years as first lady ended when FDR died unexpectedly in 1945, barely months into his fourth

64
00:07:15.653 --> 00:07:20.588
term. I've heard there were constant rumors you'd run for

65
00:07:20.589 --> 00:07:27.768
office. ELEANOR ROOSEVELT: Although I campaigned for Democratic presidential candidates, I had no interest in running

66
00:07:27.769 --> 00:07:33.957
for anything. From 1945 to 1952, I served as a delegate to the United

67
00:07:33.958 --> 00:07:40.482
Nations, where my proudest accomplishment was the passage of the Universal Declaration of Human Rights.

68
00:07:40.483 --> 00:07:46.637
Those rights ranged from free speech and privacy to health and education.

69
00:07:46.638 --> 00:07:53.671
FAYE MOSS: Wow! You certainly led a full life, but it's time to let you get back to

70
00:07:53.672 --> 00:07:58.976
history. ELEANOR ROOSEVELT: Thank you

71
00:07:58.977 --> 00:08:03.234
for having me! FAYE MOSS: Eleanor Roosevelt continued working

72
00:08:03.235 --> 00:08:11.986
with the United Nations. In the early 1960s, President John F. Kennedy named her chairperson of his committee on the

73
00:08:11.987 --> 00:08:18.132
status of women. She kept writing, speaking, and traveling the world for causes

74
00:08:18.133 --> 00:08:27.127
she believed in. Her final "My Day" column was published six weeks before she died in 1962 at the age

75
00:08:27.128 --> 00:08:32.936
of 78. Today she is remembered as one of the most influential women of the twentieth

76
00:08:32.937 --> 00:08:44.914
century. I hope you enjoyed today's show. See you next time on Superstars in History.

